Year = {2024},
Volume = {170},
Month = {JUN},
Abstract = {Prognostic models provide an avenue to predict the risk of individual
   patients and support shared-decision making. Many prognostic models are
   published annually, and systematic reviews provide an avenue to collate
   the existing evidence behind prognostic models to determine whether a
   model demonstrates adequate predictive performance and is ready for
   real-world use. This article provides a brief step-by-step guide on how
   to conduct a systematic review and meta-analysis of prognostic model
   studies and how these reviews differ from systematic reviews of therapy
   and diagnosis. (c) 2024 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier Inc. This
   is an open access article under the CC BY license
